STAGE 14 — ADVERSARIAL DESTRUCTION PASS (final)
The adversarial pass asks: what is the single strongest reason NOT to lock this mark?

Trunveld — strongest reason NOT to lock
The -veld terminal is a common Dutch noun and a productive Germanic toponym suffix (-feld). In an EUIPO filing this creates two distinct risks:

Absolute grounds — a Dutch or German examiner could raise a suggestive/geographic-suggestive objection depending on how the specification is drafted.
Relative grounds — the corridor of [X]veld / [X]feld marks is populated in DE/NL business registries; a phonetic/material-root opposition from an existing -veld/-feld mark holder in adjacent classes is a live risk. Neither risk is dispositive without authenticated registry search — but neither is dismissible.
Umtrayen — strongest reason NOT to lock
Hearing-to-spelling instability in English is a first-order commercial defect for an infrastructure brand whose primary sales motion is developer word-of-mouth and conference/podcast mention. A brand that cannot survive being said aloud once and typed correctly is structurally weakened regardless of trademark strength. Additionally, the pseudo-toponym drift in Romance languages creates minor first-impression noise in EU markets.

Neither survivor is destroyed at this stage — but both carry a substantive strongest-reason-not-to-lock, which is precisely what §15 requires to be surfaced honestly.

